WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.337
(paper airplane whooshing)
(graphic booming)

00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:06.837
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:12.848 --> 00:00:13.780
- Through all these experiences

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.491
that you've had in the
past couple of years,

00:00:15.491 --> 00:00:18.015
is there something that sticks out to you

00:00:18.015 --> 00:00:21.122
that's been a learning
moment or a growing moment?

00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:23.643
Whether it's an event or
whether it's just this thought

00:00:23.643 --> 00:00:25.854
that passed your mind
that's really stuck out

00:00:25.854 --> 00:00:27.404
to you as something formative

00:00:27.404 --> 00:00:31.275
and directing you to
where you're going now?

00:00:31.275 --> 00:00:33.595
- The two super-formative ones for me

00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:35.845
happened when I was 20-ish.

00:00:36.918 --> 00:00:40.245
One was I was in Paris
and I was a tourist,

00:00:40.245 --> 00:00:43.240
and I'm sitting outside
the Notre Dame cathedral

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.967
and I have my map, I'm
trying to figure out

00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:46.620
where I'm going next and by myself,

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.581
and this French girl comes up to me

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.225
and asks me for directions.

00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:51.474
And I'm thinking, "Why is a French girl

00:00:51.474 --> 00:00:52.674
asking me for directions?"

00:00:52.674 --> 00:00:53.637
Like, it doesn't really, right?

00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:56.082
Like, "Hmm, I should ask
this girl out, I got to,

00:00:56.082 --> 00:00:58.403
let's see what she's doing
tonight and take her to coffee,

00:00:58.403 --> 00:00:59.566
something like that, right?

00:00:59.566 --> 00:01:00.691
And I chicken out, right?

00:01:00.691 --> 00:01:02.373
I chicken out, I'm worried about my French

00:01:02.373 --> 00:01:04.695
not being good enough or whatever reason.

00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:07.819
So she walks away and I'm pissed
that I didn't take my shot,

00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:09.269
not that it would've been amazing

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:12.846
but not knowing is worse
than the rejection.

00:01:12.846 --> 00:01:15.162
And so I tried to take her picture

00:01:15.162 --> 00:01:18.190
from across the river
as she was walking by

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:19.356
and she actually kind of walked

00:01:19.356 --> 00:01:20.973
behind the stalls so I didn't get her,

00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:24.256
but as soon as I got home
I put that on my wall.

00:01:24.256 --> 00:01:26.957
I put that picture on
my wall as a reminder

00:01:26.957 --> 00:01:31.457
to the next time I'm
afraid, I just got to do it.

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:33.760
I just got to say yes and figure it out

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.939
because not knowing is
worse than getting a no.

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:39.497
Like, that short term no

00:01:39.497 --> 00:01:42.839
is okay compared to a
lifetime of not knowing.

00:01:42.839 --> 00:01:46.021
And so I looked at that
everyday and I didn't actually

00:01:46.021 --> 00:01:48.446
tell the story to my friends
and people who came over

00:01:48.446 --> 00:01:50.188
because, again, I was
embarrassed and ashamed,

00:01:50.188 --> 00:01:52.445
they just see this beautiful
French riverside picture.

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.880
But to me it's like,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.421
"I missed my shot man!
(interviewers laughing)

00:01:55.421 --> 00:01:56.620
Next time I'm not going to miss my shot."

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.118
You're like Eminem, you got one shot,

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.771
one opportunity, right?
- Yeah, I know, I know.

00:01:59.771 --> 00:02:01.551
- To seize everything
you ever wanted, right?

00:02:01.551 --> 00:02:03.558
You going to seize it or not?
- Got you.

00:02:03.558 --> 00:02:05.881
- And so that saved me
because I said I wanted

00:02:05.881 --> 00:02:08.678
to be a banker and my yearbook
said I'm going to be a banker,

00:02:08.678 --> 00:02:11.342
I had that opportunity when
I'm making $300 a month

00:02:11.342 --> 00:02:13.554
in my company and just sucking, right?

00:02:13.554 --> 00:02:15.265
Like, not making anything.

00:02:15.265 --> 00:02:16.904
And I get these opportunities to work

00:02:16.904 --> 00:02:20.080
at Merrill Lynch and McKinsey consulting,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.994
$80-100,000 starting salary,
flying me to New York,

00:02:22.994 --> 00:02:24.658
putting me up in the Waldorf Astoria.

00:02:24.658 --> 00:02:27.341
The whole deal, wine and
dine, what I thought I wanted,

00:02:27.341 --> 00:02:29.994
the jobs that my friends weren't getting.

00:02:29.994 --> 00:02:31.381
Like, what do I do?

00:02:31.381 --> 00:02:33.766
Do I take this crappy company
that's not going anywhere

00:02:33.766 --> 00:02:36.061
or do I take this dream
job that I always wanted?

00:02:36.061 --> 00:02:40.812
And I remembered the picture
and what am I going to regret?

00:02:40.812 --> 00:02:44.793
And I would regret not trying the company.

00:02:44.793 --> 00:02:47.611
I knew that in a year,
I could get another job.

00:02:47.611 --> 00:02:49.042
It may not be that job,

00:02:49.042 --> 00:02:51.067
but it could be a similar job,
- Absolutely.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:51.960
- but I wouldn't know if I would

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.350
get this company opportunity again.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.654
So I had to go for it and be like,

00:02:55.654 --> 00:02:58.415
"I'm okay with it without
working, but I need to know

00:02:58.415 --> 00:02:59.723
because I don't want to
live the rest of my life

00:02:59.723 --> 00:03:01.000
not knowing that, you know what,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.746
if I gave it a little
bit more on that thing

00:03:03.746 --> 00:03:05.130
then maybe it would've worked out."

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.929
And so that failure in
Paris with that girl

00:03:07.929 --> 00:03:09.394
and putting that picture up on the wall

00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:11.939
to remind myself with my failure,

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:14.694
that gave me the spark
to then better myself

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:16.882
the next time and just be okay.

00:03:16.882 --> 00:03:20.051
And so that's what all
my decisions are through,

00:03:20.051 --> 00:03:22.308
always trying to minimize regret.

00:03:22.308 --> 00:03:24.370
Like, what am I not going to regret?

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.937
And the fear of regret
can help you push through

00:03:26.937 --> 00:03:29.578
the short-term fear of whatever decision

00:03:29.578 --> 00:03:32.356
that you need to make short-term.

00:03:32.356 --> 00:03:33.404
And then the other formative lesson

00:03:33.404 --> 00:03:35.035
was around that same time, coming back,

00:03:35.035 --> 00:03:36.860
just figuring out how to
make my business grow.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.289
And I told my business partner I quit

00:03:38.289 --> 00:03:40.124
and it was like the worst day of my life.

00:03:40.124 --> 00:03:42.157
I realized that I'm not
the first guy that tried

00:03:42.157 --> 00:03:45.156
to sell software before
and by modeling success,

00:03:45.156 --> 00:03:46.772
like somebody else has
already figured this out,

00:03:46.772 --> 00:03:48.520
I don't have to come up
with all the ideas my self.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.779
And I looked at how Bill
Gates started Microsoft.

00:03:50.779 --> 00:03:53.539
Zero to one, like not how he
makes an extra million now,

00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:56.492
but zero to one, how do you do
that? That's what I want to do.

00:03:56.492 --> 00:03:58.716
And I found this story and I researched it

00:03:58.716 --> 00:04:01.299
and I was making $300 a month

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:03.384
and within two months or so

00:04:03.384 --> 00:04:07.561
I made my first deal that was
13 and a half thousand dollars

00:04:07.561 --> 00:04:10.555
and that doesn't sound like
a lot of bank but, dude,

00:04:10.555 --> 00:04:14.012
for me that was sick!
- Trust me we know.

00:04:14.012 --> 00:04:16.269
- That was the deal, man!
- Absolutely.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:19.517
- 13 and a half K, what? That's crazy!

00:04:19.517 --> 00:04:22.236
Like this could actually work.
(interviewers laughing)

00:04:22.236 --> 00:04:23.289
And more important than that,

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:26.245
I had a model I could repeat,
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:26.245 --> 00:04:27.316
- so it's not just a one time deal,

00:04:27.316 --> 00:04:28.942
I could sell it again and again and again.

00:04:28.942 --> 00:04:31.647
And so that idea of, so the first one,

00:04:31.647 --> 00:04:34.213
living with no regrets,
trying as much as possible.

00:04:34.213 --> 00:04:35.813
And then two, whenever
I'm faced with a problem

00:04:35.813 --> 00:04:38.372
I don't know what to do,
I try to model success.

00:04:38.372 --> 00:04:39.998
Find someone who has already done it

00:04:39.998 --> 00:04:41.432
and what can I learn from that?

00:04:41.432 --> 00:04:43.194
Like, what am I doing
on my YouTube channel?

00:04:43.194 --> 00:04:44.298
It's just modeling success,

00:04:44.298 --> 00:04:46.386
it's just finding people
who've done great things,

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:48.242
and it's selfish for me.

00:04:48.242 --> 00:04:50.185
I would do the YouTube channel

00:04:50.185 --> 00:04:52.452
even if you guys didn't like it, for me.

00:04:52.452 --> 00:04:54.351
It's my growth and my development.

00:04:54.351 --> 00:04:57.499
I want to learn from
Kobe and all these guys

00:04:57.499 --> 00:04:59.777
that we put up on the channel.

00:04:59.777 --> 00:05:02.492
So I'm fortunate I get to
surround myself with that daily.

00:05:02.492 --> 00:05:03.822
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:05:03.822 --> 00:05:05.978
Thank you to the guys from VNR Media

00:05:05.978 --> 00:05:07.340
